WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.800
Slicing and Python is an easy way to create sub lists from larger lists.

00:06.800 --> 00:13.040
So we saw how easy it is to access an item by index.

00:14.240 --> 00:20.240
We can also ask for a range at an index with slicing.

00:21.280 --> 00:25.920
So let's say I had a string here.

00:26.000 --> 00:35.200
If I got the value at zero, that would just be the letter H.

00:35.200 --> 00:46.160
I can ask for a slice of the string by passing in the starting point followed by a colon and then the ending point.

00:46.160 --> 00:52.960
So that's just going to return everything from index seven and onwards.

00:52.960 --> 00:55.840
You can also do lopsided slicing.

00:57.360 --> 01:07.360
So if this first value was not present, that is a shortcut saying start at zero.

01:12.080 --> 01:18.880
If we have a value on the left hand side, but not on the right hand side, that's a shortcut that says

01:19.520 --> 01:24.480
go all the way to the end of the string or the end of the sequence.

01:26.320 --> 01:30.720
So that's a little bit of shortcut syntax that you might see.

01:30.720 --> 01:40.240
If you quickly want to create a full copy of a string or a list, you can omit both the start and the end.

01:40.960 --> 01:45.280
And that will return just a full copy from the beginning to the end.

01:45.840 --> 01:50.320
You can also have negative indexing.

01:50.320 --> 01:59.840
So if I wanted just the last character, I could pass in negative one and that says go back one from the end.

02:01.040 --> 02:05.840
You can also do a little bit of reversing here with negative indexing.

02:05.840 --> 02:15.040
So I can say from negative 10 to negative seven and that will get the values from

02:16.000 --> 02:23.600
the 10th index from last to the 7th index from last, if I switched the order of these.

02:25.760 --> 02:33.840
Oops, then you would not see anything at all because that's asking for a slice that doesn't exist.

02:33.840 --> 02:43.520
You can also pass in a stride or a step as well that will be an optional third argument here.

02:44.160 --> 02:50.160
I'm not going to cover it. You generally won't see it too much out there in code in the wild,

02:50.160 --> 02:52.720
but in case you do, you know what to look for.

02:53.520 --> 02:59.920
There's another optional exercise on the zip function that lets you take two different lists

02:59.920 --> 03:07.520
and combine them together to form some data structures that might help you translate into

03:07.520 --> 03:13.200
a dictionary if needed. Some more advanced concept if that's something that you find interesting.

03:13.200 --> 03:19.200
I recommend going back and checking that out, but we're going to talk about working with files.

